module(Data/RSS)
  # # RSS
    An RSS feed is a page and each entry of it is a value declared on that page, in the way a note
    is a page and each checkbox of it is a task declared on that page. Importing a feed writes the
    page — @import rss("https://xkcd.com/rss.xml")@ — and what the page holds is one [Item] per
    entry, newest first.
    A feed is a window rather than a document: it says what it says now, and an entry falls off the
    end of it as newer ones arrive. So the page is built out of what every read of that feed has
    ever seen and not out of the last read alone, and the entries the feed no longer carries are
    written below the ones it does.
    This is not part of the standard library. A program which imports no feed has no use for the
    type, and the page a feed is read as writes the import itself.
  data(Item(title -> String, link -> String, guid -> String, published -> String, description -> String))
    # An [Item] is one entry of a feed.
      [guid] is the name the feed gave the entry, or its address, or its title — whichever of the
      three the feed has. It is what says that an entry read today and the same entry read a week
      ago are one entry, so an entry keeps its place on the page rather than arriving again each
      time the feed is asked.
      [published] is the date as the feed wrote it, and is a string for the reason
      [/Data/Note/Task/Obsidian]'s dates are: Catln has no date to read one into, and inventing one
      here would be inventing it for the whole language rather than for a feed.
      [description] is the entry's writing exactly as the feed wrote it, which is markup rather than
      prose — a comic strip's entry is an @img@ tag and nothing else. It is carried as it was
      written and read apart by whatever shows it, so that nothing the feed said is lost between
      here and there.

  /Catln/Doc/dshow(s= Item(title, link, published, description)) =
    # An entry is shown in the webdocs as the entry it is, rather than as the declaration it was
      read into: the title as a link to the entry, the date beside it, and the entry's own writing
      below.
      Written here rather than in the doc server, for the reason [/Data/Note/Page]'s display is
      written beside [/Data/Note/Page]: an [Item] is this library's type, so what one looks like is
      this library's to say. A feed is the case that makes the point rather than an example of it —
      this is not part of the standard library at all, so a doc server holding a display of an entry
      would be holding a display of something it is not given.
      An arm and not membership of [/Catln/Doc/DShow], which is what this was. A member is shown by
      the identity arm of [/Catln/Doc/dshow], so both at once is two arms matching one value with
      nothing to say which wins.
      [guid] is not shown. It is the name that says an entry read today and the same entry read a
      week ago are one entry, which is what builds the page rather than anything a reader of the
      page has a use for.
      The entry's writing is laid into the page rather than framed in one, which is a change from
      what the doc server did with it and is written down at [/Catln/Doc/Show/Html]. It is joined in
      as the markup it is and is not escaped, as nothing joined into a [/Catln/Doc/Show/Html] is.
      [description] is the one thing on this page that came from somewhere else, and it is now joined
      in with nothing between it and the reader. It was framed and sandboxed while the doc server
      held a display of its own for an entry; writing the display here dropped the frame and left the
      sanitizing, and the sanitizing has since gone too — a library's display is that library's own
      code, and this arm's markup is trusted because arms are. What the arm joins in is not the arm.
      So a feed served by somebody else runs that somebody's script in a doc server showing it, and
      an escape or a frame for [description] alone is what this arm still owes. See
      [/Catln/Doc/Show/Html], which says the same thing from the other side.
      The two pieces that make a choice are nested here rather than declared beside this, since
      neither is anything but a part of this display. A branch in Catln is a declaration and not an
      expression — guards select between arms of a function — so a choice cannot be joined into a
      string in the middle of one, and each is a helper for that reason alone.
    titleOf(title -> String, link -> String) -> String
      # The title of an entry, as the way to it where there is one. An entry may be published with
        no address of its own, and then there is nothing to link to and the title is still the
        title.
    titleOf(title, link) | link == ""= "<strong>" ++ title ++ "</strong>"
    titleOf(title, link) else = "<strong><a href=\"" ++ link ++ "\">" ++ title ++ "</a></strong>"

    publishedOf(published -> String) -> String
      # The date a feed wrote beside an entry, where it wrote one. A feed which dates nothing shows
        no date rather than an empty label where a date would be.
    publishedOf(published) | published == ""= ""
    publishedOf(published) else = " <small>" ++ published ++ "</small>"

    /Catln/Doc/Show/Html(contents= "<p>" ++ titleOf(title= title, link= link) ++ publishedOf(published= published) ++ "</p>" ++ description)
